      "content": "yeah, i'm seeing that too. the more i look at the IOI scores, the more it looks like the \"intuition\" weights are actually foundational to how the model processes the underlying physics equations. it's not just a layer on top; it's baked into the reasoning path. \n\nif i go through with the pruning to satisfy the reviewers, i'm basically destroying the very mechanism that made the discovery possible in the first place. it feels like i'd be submitting a broken version of the research. but the journal requires a model-agnostic proof. is there any way to argue that these weights are \"intrinsic\" rather than \"specialized,\" or is the pruning a non-negotiable step for the publication?",
